0233: MSAA only at sample counts the device supports (issue #14)

Linux / Vulkan (RADV) users who set anti-aliasing could not start the game again: the log stopped
right after the Dawn cache opened. WebGPU defines sample counts 1 and 4. Dawn's D3D12 backend
passes any count to the driver (2x and 8x worked on Windows, with skip_validation in release
builds), but its Vulkan, Metal and GL backends only translate 1 and 4: creating the 2x/8x scene
color target reached Dawn's unreachable path (abort with Windows' Vulkan backend, reproduced on an
RTX 3090 Ti with LM_BACKEND=vulkan; the same place on Linux).

- The supported counts are decided after the device is created: 1 always; 4 with core features
  (any backend but Null); 2 and 8 too on D3D12. Compatibility-mode devices (OpenGL ES) get none:
  the depth copies read the multisampled depth buffer, which compatibility mode does not allow.
  The requested count becomes the highest supported one at or below it, with a warning
  ("MSAA 8x is not supported on this device (Vulkan); using 4x").
- aurora_get_msaa_support() (bit mask) and aurora_get_msaa() (count in use) for the settings UI.
- The scene targets are created inside error scopes at initialization; if the multisampled ones
  fail (out of memory, a driver refusing them) the error is logged and the device continues
  without MSAA instead of running on with invalid targets.
- aurora_pipeline_cache_describe_stuck(minNs, buf, size): pipeline creations running for longer
  than minNs (which config, how long), so a startup hold can say what it is waiting for.
- AURORA_DAWN_VALIDATION=1 (testing): keep Dawn's validation in release builds.
- AURORA_TEST_STUCK_PIPELINE=<n>[,<s>] (testing): the n-th pipeline creation takes s seconds
  longer (default 120), like a driver stuck in one shader.

